Bernard Helffer ... We call this assertion the \\emph{Extended Courant Property}.\\smallskipIn this paper, we propose simple and explicit examples for which the extended Courant property is false: convex domains in $\\R^n$ (hypercube and equilateral triangle), domains with cracks in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, on the round sphere $\\mathbb{S}^2$, and on a flat torus $\\mathbb{T}^2$.\nTitle: On Courant's nodal domain property for linear combinations of eigenfunctions, Part I\nAuthors: Pierre Bérard, Bernard Helffer\nDate: Wed May 10 12:40:16 2017\n\nAccording to Courant's theorem, an eigenfunction as\\-sociated with the $n$-th eigenvalue $\\lambda\\_n$ has at most $n$ nodal domains. A footnote in the book of Courant and Hilbert, states that the same assertion is true for any linear combination of eigenfunctions associated with eigenvalues less than or equal to $\\lambda\\_n$. We call this assertion the \\emph{Extended Courant Property}.\\smallskipIn this paper, we propose simple and explicit examples for which the extended Courant property is false: convex domains in $\\R^n$ (hypercube and equilateral triangle), domains with cracks in $\\mathbb{R}^2$, on the round sphere $\\mathbb{S}^2$, and on a flat torus $\\mathbb{T}^2$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForum of Mathematics, Sigma (2026), Vol. 14:e67 1–9 (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/DF39434D8B16CAF4725048FA3B450D08/S2050509426102126a.pdf/block_sizes_in_the_block_sets_conjecture.pdf)\nciteturn7search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Forum of Mathematics, Sigma (2026), Vol. 14:e67 1–9 ... ‘blocks in large products’, introduced by Leader, Russell and Walters. ... that every transitive set (a set whose symmetry group acts transitively) is Euclidean Ramsey. ... A ﬁnite set X in some Euclidean space R𝑑is called Ramsey if for any k there exists an n such that ... A rival conjecture was formulated by Leader, Russell and Walters in [8].\nForum of Mathematics, Sigma (2026), Vol. 14:e67 1–9\ndoi:10.1017/fms.2026.10212\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\nBlock sizes in the block sets conjecture\nMaria-Romina Ivan\n1, Imre Leader2 and Mark Walters3\n1University of Cambridge, United Kingdom; E-mail: mri25@cam.ac.uk (Corresponding author).\n2University of Cambridge, United Kingdom; E-mail: leader@maths.cam.ac.uk.\n3Queen Mary University of London, United Kingdom; E-mail: m.walters@qmul.ac.uk.\nReceived: 4 June 2024; Accepted: 21 February 2026\n2020 Mathematical Subject Classiﬁcation: Primary – 05D10; Secondary – 05C55\nAbstract\nA set X is called Euclidean Ramsey if, for any k and suﬃciently large n, every k-colouring of R𝑛contains a\nmonochromatic congruent copy of X. This notion was introduced by Erdős, Graham, Montgomery, Rothschild,\nSpencer and Straus. They asked if a set is Ramsey if and only if it is spherical, meaning that it lies on the surface\nof a sphere. It is not too diﬃcult to show that if a set is not spherical then it is not Euclidean Ramsey either, but the\nA ﬁnite set X in some Euclidean space R𝑑is called Ramsey if for any k there exists an n such that\nwhenever R𝑛is k-coloured there is a monochromatic copy (where ‘copy’ means congruent copy) of X.\nThe study of Ramsey sets was initiated by Erdős, Graham, Montgomery, Rothschild, Spencer and Straus\n[2]. They gave examples of Ramsey sets, and proved that a nonspherical set cannot be Ramsey – here a\nset is spherical if it is a subset of some sphere. They conjectured that this is the only obstruction to a set\nbeing Ramsey, in other words that X is Ramsey if and only if X is spherical.\nProgress on this question has been very slow. Frankl and Rödl [4] showed that every triangle is\nRamsey, and then that every simplex is Ramsey [5]. Kříž [7] showed that every regular n-gon is Ramsey,\nand in fact he showed some more general versions of this statement, which imply for example that every\nPlatonic solid is Ramsey.\nA rival conjecture was formulated by Leader, Russell and Walters in [8]. They conjectured that a set\nis Ramsey if and only if it is a subset of some ﬁnite transitive set (perhaps living in more dimensions),\n© The Author(s), 2026. Published by Cambridge University Press. This is an Open Access article, distributed under the terms of the Creative\nCommons Attribution licence (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0), which permits unrestricted re-use, distribution and reproduction,\nprovided the original article is properly cited.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1017/fms.2026.10212 Published online by Cambridge University Press--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Bijection for Partitions with Initial Repetitions (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=20c8de77125fe1c1fb0825ac1a211b825b416436&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn7search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; A Bijection for Partitions with Initial Repetitions ... Keith ... a proof via bijection of the two sets and kindly noted that before publication\nA Bijection for Partitions with Initial Repetitions\nWilliam J. Keith\nUniversity of Lisbon, Av. Prof. Gama Pinto 2, Lisbon, Portugal\nwjk150@cii.fc.ul.pt\nOctober 14, 2010\nAbstract\nA theorem of Andrews equates partitions in which no part is repeated\nmore than 2k−1 times to partitions in which, if j appears at least k times,\nall parts less than j also do so. This paper proves the theorem bijectively,\nwith some of the generalizations that usually arise from such proofs.\n1\nIntroduction\nIn [1], George Andrews proved the q-series identity\n∞\nY\n∞\nX\nqk·1+k·2+···+k·n\n(1 −q)(1 −q2) · · · (1 −qn)\nn=0\nj=n+1\n(1 + qj + q2j + · · · + q(k−1)j)\n∞\nY\n=\nj=1\n(1 + qj + q2j + · · · + q(2k−1)j)\n(1)\narXiv:1010.2653v1  [math.CO]  13 Oct 2010\nwhich can be interpreted combinatorially as stating that partitions of n in which\nno part is repeated more than 2k −1 times (the right-hand side) are equinu-\nmerous with partitions of n in which, if any part j appears at least k times\nas a part, then any part of size less than j also appears at least k times as a\npart. These latter he named partitions with initial k-repetitions. He requested\na proof via bijection of the two sets and kindly noted that before publication\nthe current author (his student at the time) was able to provide. That proof\nhas not previously appeared in print and the details were recently requested, so\nit is oﬀered here for the literature.\nAs is typical of bijective proofs, the map can modestly generalize the theorem\nby careful observation of the characteristics of the starting and ending partitions.\n2\nThe Bijection\nWe assume familiarity with the common vocabulary of partitions; see [2] for the\nstandard reference.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA bijection for partitions simultaneously $s$-regular and $t$-distinct (https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.13840)\nciteturn7academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Title: A bijection for partitions simultaneously $s$-regular and $t$-distinct ... Keith ... As a generalized version of Glaisher's bijection, the map may be widely useful in other partition combinatorics.\nTitle: A bijection for partitions simultaneously $s$-regular and $t$-distinct\nAuthors: William J. Keith\nDate: Thu Jul 28 00:51:49 2022\n\nIn this note a bijection is constructed between the set of partitions of n simultaneously s-regular and t-distinct, and those simultaneously t-regular and s-distinct. Some implications of the map are discussed. As a generalized version of Glaisher's bijection, the map may be widely useful in other partition combinatorics. A previous conjecture concerning iterations of Glaisher's bijection is given a counterexample.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCurriculum Vitae of William J. Keith, Dec 2022 (https://pages.mtu.edu/~wjkeith/CVDec2022.pdf)\nciteturn7search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2