ients\n15. July 2018, Combinatory Analysis 2018, Pennsylvania State University\n(Invited): Unimodality of q-Binomial Diﬀerences via Kirillov/Reshetihkin\n4--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConference on „Recent Results on Quantum Many-Body Systems“ (https://www.mathematik.uni-muenchen.de/~nam/Abstract.pdf)\nciteturn7search19 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Bernard Helffer, On the extended Courant nodal property: examples and ... Bérard.\nChristian Hainzl, The interacting Fermi gas: A step beyond Hartree-Fock  \nAbstract: We consider a homogenous Fermi gas interacting via a regular mean-field \npotential. We recover the correlation energy to leading order in the coupling parameter.   \n \nBernard Helffer, On the extended Courant nodal property: examples and \nconterexamples   \n  \nAbstract: In a footnote of Courant-Hilbert, it is claimed that Courant nodal theorem extends \nto linear combination of eigenfunctions. This statement is correct in dimension 1 (Theorem of \nSturm) but as observed by V. Arnold in the seventies would imply wrong statements in  \nAlgebraic Geometry. In this talk, we would like to present very simple examples contradicting \nCourant-Hilbert's statement and discuss alternative statements. This is a work in \ncollaboration with P. Bérard.  \nDirk Hundertmark, Cwikel's bound reloaded   \nAbstract: There are a couple of proofs by now for the famous Cwikel Lieb  \nRozenblum  (CLR) bound, which is a semiclassical bound on the number of bound states for \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations (https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026)\nciteturn7academia20 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: A classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations ... Date: Wed Sep 23 00:00:00 2026 ... In particular, some Ramsey cyclic quadrilaterals are not subtransitive, disproving the necessity direction of the Leader–Russell–Walters conjectured characterization.\nTitle: A classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations\nAuthors: OpenAI\nDate: Wed Sep 23 00:00:00 2026\n\nWe classify finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations by a necessary and sufficient tensor condition over their coordinate fields. The Ramsey property here concerns monochromatic congruent copies at the original scale under arbitrary finite colorings. The criterion shows that every nonempty subtransitive set and every nonempty set of at most five points on a circle is Ramsey. In particular, some Ramsey cyclic quadrilaterals are not subtransitive, disproving the necessity direction of the Leader–Russell–Walters conjectured characterization.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNon-boundedness of the number of super level domains of eigenfunctions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.03668)\nciteturn7academia21 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; Authors: Pierre Bérard, Philippe Charron, Bernard Helffer ... Generalizing Courant's nodal domain theorem, the \"Extended Courant property\" is the statement that a linear combination of the first $n$ eigenfunctions has at most $n$ nodal domains.\nTitle: Non-boundedness of the number of super level domains of eigenfunctions\nAuthors: Pierre Bérard, Philippe Charron, Bernard Helffer\nDate: Sun Jun  9 16:43:16 2019\n\nGeneralizing Courant's nodal domain theorem, the \"Extended Courant property\" is the statement that a linear combination of the first $n$ eigenfunctions has at most $n$ nodal domains. A related question is to estimate the number of connected components of the (super) level sets of a Neumann eigenfunction $u$. Indeed, in this case, the first eigenfunction is constant, and looking at the level sets of $u$ amounts to looking at the nodal sets $\\{u-a=0\\}$, where $a$ is a real constant. In the first part of the paper, we prove that the Extended Courant property is false for the subequilateral triangle and for regular $N$-gons ($N$ large), with the Neumann boundary condition. More precisely, we prove that there exists a Neumann eigenfunction $u_k$ of the $N$-gon, with labelling $k$, $4 \\le k \\le 6$, such that the set $\\{u_k \\not = 1\\}$ has $(N+1)$ connected components. In the second part, we prove that there exists a metric $g$ on $\\mathbb{T}^2$ (resp. on $\\mathbb{S}^2$), which can be chosen arbitrarily close to the flat metric (resp. round metric), and an eigenfunction $u$ of the associated Laplace-Beltrami operator, such that the set $\\{u \\not = 1\\}$ has infinitely many connected components. In particular the Extended Courant property is false for these closed surfaces. These results are strongly motivated by a recent paper by Buhovsky, Logunov and Sodin. As for the positive direction, in Appendix~B, we prove that the Extended Courant property is true for the isotropic quantum harmonic oscillator in $\\mathbb{R}^2$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations · oai-math-revised (https://mathcommunity.github.io/oai-math-revised/papers/A-classification-of-finite-Euclidean-Ramsey-configurations-September-23-2026/)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; In particular, some Ramsey cyclic quadrilaterals are not subtransitive, disproving the necessity direction of the Leader–Russell–Walters conjectured characterization. ...   * Will Strinz, Möbius invariance of the Ramsey property for finite spherical sets (GitHub mobius-ramsey; Palomar entry PALOMAR-2026-10-14-000008, 2026-10-14). Lean development deriving from the classification that the Ramsey property of finite unit-sphere sets is invariant under Möbius maps (modelled by the Lorentz group), reusing the release's Lean proof; the README states no human expert has reviewed it. ... Umbrella repository with Lean proofs, exact certificates, numerical probes and a conjecture ledger on which finite spherical sets are Ramsey; it explicitly trusts the release's Lean proof of the classification.\n\n# A classification of finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations\n\nOpenAI · September 23, 2026\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe classify finite Euclidean Ramsey configurations by a necessary and sufficient tensor condition over their coordinate fields. The Ramsey property here concerns monochromatic congruent copies at the original scale under arbitrary finite colorings. The criterion shows that every nonempty subtransitive set and every nonempty set of at most five points on a circle is Ramsey. In particular, some Ramsey cyclic quadrilaterals are not subtransitive, disproving the necessity direction of the Leader–Russell–Walters conjectured characterization.\n\nPDF of the manuscript\n\nIf the PDF does not display here, open it directly: Open PDF.\n\n## Notes\n\nNo notes yet. Add a reference, a comment or your name with the buttons above, or edit NOTES.md on GitHub.\n\n## Follow-up works\n\n  * JD Jones, Six concyclic points satisfy the Euclidean Ramsey tensor criterion, as does every spherical set of at most m + 4 points of affine dimension m and every algebraic spherical set (GitHub six-concyclic-lean; Palomar entry PALOMAR-2026-10-07-000011, 2026-10-07). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLeader–Russell–Walters Hales–Jewett conjecture for finite groups | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/342698/leader-russell-walters-hales-jewett-conjecture-for-finite-gr)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Leader–Russell–Walters Hales–Jewett conjecture for finite groups ... Leader–Russell–Walters Hales–Jewett conjecture. ... Vassilis Kanellopoulos and Miltiadis Karamanlis, “A Hales–Jewett type property of finite solvable groups”, arXiv:1905.04892 (2019).\n\n# Leader–Russell–Walters Hales–Jewett conjecture for finite groups\n\nAbout 7 years old · traced to 2019\n\nLet $G$G be a finite group and let $r\\in\\mathbb{N}$r∈N. For a nonempty subset $H\\subseteq G$H⊆G, an $H$H-variable word over $G$G is a word whose variables are indexed by elements of $H$H; its length is the number of coordinates and its degree is the number of variable positions. For $g\\in G$g∈G, write $W(g)$W(g) for the constant word obtained by replacing each variable $v_h$v h​ by $hg$h g. An $r$r-coloring of $G^N$G N is a map from $G^N$G N to $[r]$[r], and a set is monochromatic when the coloring is constant on it.\n\nLeader–Russell–Walters Hales–Jewett conjecture. There exist positive integers $d$d and $N$N such that, for every $r$r-coloring of $G^N$G N, there exist a nonempty $H\\subseteq G$H⊆G and an $H$H-variable word $W$W over $G$G of length $N$N and degree $d$d for which\n\n$$\\{W(g):g\\in G\\}$${W(g):g∈G}\n\nis monochromatic.\n\nThis is the purely combinatorial conjecture used by Leader, Russell and Walters to study the subtransitivity direction of their Euclidean Ramsey conjecture. It resembles the Hales–Jewett theorem; the paper proves the corresponding property for finite solvable groups, while the assertion for arbitrary finite groups remains open in the supplied text.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nVassilis Kanellopoulos and Miltiadis Karamanlis, “A Hales–Jewett type property of finite solvable groups”, arXiv:1905.04892 (2019).\n\nfinite-groupshales-jewett-theoremramsey-theory\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nModulo 2 congruences for partitions with initial repetitions | Boletín de la Sociedad Matemática Mexicana | S